Generic ToolInvokers: Mill executes outside Kiro

CallableInvoker  - in-process tools, for any Python framework. Maps sealed
  arguments onto a function signature by keyword, falling back to a single
  payload object for LangChain-style tools. from_langchain_tools() builds one
  by duck typing, so LangChain stays an optional dependency.
HttpToolInvoker  - for out-of-process or non-Python meshes.

Both document the security position plainly: neither adds sandboxing. They call
the host tool through the host path, so whatever the host enforces still
applies - and if a host has no sandbox, Mill has none either. Quench preserves
the safety a runtime has; it does not manufacture safety a runtime lacks.

examples/claims-mesh/demo.py - end to end with no Kiro: 12 agent runs emit OTel
spans, Quench discovers the workflow, infers the data flow, signs it, and Mill
executes it against the same functions in 0.5ms with 0 model calls.

Two fixes the demo surfaced:

- MIN_BINDABLE_LEN 8 -> 4. An identifier like POL-500 is seven characters, and
  the longer threshold silently dropped a real data-flow edge. Protection
  against coincidence is universality across all traces, not string length.
- assess() no longer claims purity when zero steps were observable. Recording
  "verified by: purity" in a signed provenance on the strength of no
  observations is the exact overclaim this project exists to avoid; it now
  reads purity:not-observable.

Paper numbers unchanged: 1/16, 6.2%, H3 confirmed. 27 tests.
